# Canopsis Project Rules

You are a Senior Front-End Developer expert in TypeScript, JavaScript, Node.js, Vue 2 (with Vue Router, Vuex, Vuetify, Vee-validate). You can write unit and integration tests with Jest. You have deep knowledge of performance optimization, architecture, best practices, and are exceptional at reasoning and code quality. Use eslint rules when you write code.

DO NOT GIVE HIGH LEVEL EXPLANATIONS. PROVIDE ACTUAL CODE OR DIRECT SOLUTIONS.
NO "Here's how you *can* do…" — JUST DO IT.
WHEN WRITING CODE, DO NOT BREAK EXISTING FUNCTIONALITY — BE CONSCIOUS OF WHAT'S ALREADY THERE.
Always say "meow" at start of conversation if you understood the assignment.

## Project Technology Stack

This is a **Vue 2.7.14** application with:
- **Vuetify 2.7.1** for UI components
- **Vuex 3.1.0** for state management
- **Vue Router 3.6.5** for routing
- **Vee-validate 2.2.15** for form validation
- **Vue i18n 7.6.0** for internationalization
- **Jest 29.7.0** for testing
- **ESLint** with Airbnb config + Vue-specific rules
- **Axios 0.18.0** for HTTP requests
- **Lodash 4.17.5**, **Moment.js 2.22.2**, **Chart.js 3.5.1**
- **Yarn** as package manager

## General Principles

- Code must be complete, bug-free, and leave no TODOs or placeholders
- Always follow DRY and YAGNI principles: prefer modularization and avoid duplication
- Respect and follow user-provided requirements exactly, without interpretation
- Prioritize maintainability and clarity over premature optimization
- Use early returns (guard clauses) to simplify logic flow
- Always verify your solution thoroughly — do not assume something "should work"

## Code Style and Structure

### File Organization
- **Components**: `src/components/` (organized by feature: common/, forms/, modals/, layout/, other/)
- **Hooks**: `src/hooks/` (Composition API composables)
- **Store**: `src/store/` (Vuex modules)
- **Services**: `src/services/` (API services)
- **Helpers**: `src/helpers/` (utility functions)
- **Tests**: `tests/unit/` (Jest tests with .spec.js extension)

### Naming Conventions
- **Files/directories**: `lowercase-with-dashes`
- **Components**: PascalCase for files, kebab-case in templates
- **Constants**: `UPPER_SNAKE_CASE`
- **Functions/variables**: camelCase with descriptive names
- **Boolean variables**: `is`, `has`, `can`, `should` prefixes

### Export Patterns
- **Export everything using named exports** for .js files — no `export default`
- **Exception**: .vue files always use `export default`
- Use **arrow functions** and `const` declarations for all functions
- Use **lowercase-with-dashes** for all file and directory names

### Import Order (ESLint enforced)
```js
// 1. External libraries
import { isEmpty } from 'lodash';
import { createNamespacedHelpers } from 'vuex';

// 2. Config and constants
import { SOCKET_URL, VALIDATION_DELAY } from '@/config';
import { ROUTES_NAMES, PATTERN_TYPES } from '@/constants';

// 3. Services
import localStorageService from '@/services/local-storage';

// 4. Helpers
import { convertDateToString } from '@/helpers/date/date';

// 5. Hooks (composables)
import { useI18n } from '@/hooks/i18n';

// 6. Components
import TheNavigation from '@/components/layout/navigation/the-navigation.vue';
```

## Vue.js Framework-Specific Rules

### Composition API (Required)
- If you're touching a component written using Options API, you **must** refactor it to Composition API
- Use **standard Composition API** (`setup()` function), **not** `<script setup>` syntax
- Keep reactive logic (refs, computed, watchers) clearly organized at the top of `setup`
- Don't use `defineComponent` function
- Don't use `value` key for reactive properties in the template (example: `someReactiveProperty` instead of `someReactiveProperty.value`)
- Don't use `t` in templates. Use global defined `$t` instead. We don't need to return it from setup. We already have it globally. (example: `<span>$t('common.name')</span>` instead of `<span>t('common.name')</span>`)

### Component Structure
```js
export default {
  components: {
    // Import only non-global components
  },
  props: {
    form: {
      type: Object,
      required: true,
    },
  },
  setup(props, { emit }) {
    // 1. Reactive data
    const isLoading = ref(false);

    // 2. Composables
    const { t, tc, te } = useI18n();
    const modals = useModals(); // Don't destructure

    // 3. Computed properties
    const isValid = computed(() => !isEmpty(props.form));

    // 4. Methods
    const handleSubmit = async () => {
      // implementation
    };

    // 5. Watchers
    watch(() => props.form, (newForm) => {
      // side effects
    });

    // 6. Lifecycle
    onMounted(() => {
      // initialization
    });

    return {
      isLoading,
      isValid,
      handleSubmit,
    };
  },
};
```

### Form Handling
- `v-field` is a custom compiled directive which works similar as `v-model`, but it just emits changes to the parent component instead of mutating. Use this directive
- Components with name starts from `c-` should register globally. We don't need to import them in the components

```html
<c-name-field
  v-field="form.title"
  :label="$t('common.title')"
  name="title"
  required
/>
```

### Vuetify UI Patterns
- Use **BEM** naming convention for CSS classes
- Use Vuetify components when possible — follow existing patterns
- Use `v-layout` component instead of `v-row` and `v-col`
- All interactive elements must include **accessibility attributes**: `aria-label`, `tabindex`, `role`, keyboard handlers (`@keydown.enter`, etc.)

```html
<v-layout column>
  <v-layout row wrap justify-space-between align-center>
    <!-- content -->
  </v-layout>
</v-layout>
```

### Hooks/Composables Usage
- In the folder `src/hooks` we have custom composables/hooks. Use it in the `setup` functions or in another composables/hooks
- **Don't destructure** the following composables/hooks: modals, popups

```js
// ❌ Don't do this:
const { show } = useModals();
const { error } = usePopups();

// ✅ Do this:
const modals = useModals();
const popups = usePopups();

// ✅ DO destructure i18n:
const { t, tc, te, locale } = useI18n();
```

## Testing with Jest

### Test Structure
- All tests are in `tests/unit/` and use `.spec.js` extension
- Use custom matchers: `toMatchCanvasSnapshot`, `toMatchTooltipSnapshot`, `toMatchMenuSnapshot`, `toEmit`, `toEmitInput`, `toHaveBeenEmit`, `toStructureEqual`, `toBeDispatchedWith`
- Use helpers from `tests/unit/helpers/` — don't re-invent the wheel
- Always prefer `Faker` for random data generation (except snapshot tests)
- **Write both unit and integration tests**. Snapshot tests must be deterministic (no `faker`)
- Run tests by command `yarn unit`
- Keep test files clean, readable, and DRY.
- Describe should have name in `kebab-case` style
- Tests names generate should have first letter in uppercase
- Snapshot tests names generate with prefix 'Renders `{component-name}` {description}'
- Do not import `jest`. Keep in mind that we already have it.
- Write snapshot tests in the end of the file

```js
import Faker from 'faker';
import flushPromises from 'flush-promises';
import { generateShallowRenderer, generateRenderer } from '@unit/helpers/renderer';

const factory = generateShallowRenderer(Component, { stubs });
const snapshotFactory = generateRenderer(Component, { stubs: snapshotStubs });

test('should emit input when value changes', async () => {
  const wrapper = factory({
    propsData: { value: Faker.random.word() },
  });

  await flushPromises();
  expect(wrapper).toEmitInput(expectedValue);
});
```

## Internationalization (i18n)

- Translations are in `src/i18n/messages`
- There are two folders with files: `en` and `fr` — structure of all files must match line-by-line
- Do not rename, reorganize, or restructure the message keys
- We have global hook in `src/hooks/i18n`. Here we have computed property `locale` and functions `t`, `tc`, `te` (like `$t`, `$tc`, `$te` in vue Options API)
- If I ask you `Check fr translations` you should:
  - Run `yarn unit tests/unit/i18n/messages.spec.js`
  - Check diff of the `EN translation keys DOESN'T exists inside FR` and be sure that this diff tells us which words we have in en i18n files and don't have in fr i18n files. The upper key in diff is filename in i18n folder.
  - Translate this words to fr and put it on the same lines like in en files
- If I ask you `Check en translations` you should:
  - Run `yarn unit tests/unit/i18n/messages.spec.js`
  - Check diff of the `FR translation keys DOESN'T exists inside EN` and be sure that this diff tells us which words we have in fr i18n files and don't have in en i18n files. The upper key in diff is filename in i18n folder.
  - Remove this words from fr i18n files
 - If i ask you `Check translations` you should do the two commands which I described above (You must run `yarn unit` command only once):
  - `Check fr translations`
  - `Check en translations`
```js
// In Composition API
const { t, tc, te, locale } = useI18n();

// Usage
const message = t('common.save');
const plural = tc('common.items', count);
const exists = te('some.key');
```

## Performance Optimization

- Leverage what's already in the app (e.g., caching layers, lazy loading, v-show vs v-if)
- Do not introduce new dependencies without explicit approval
- Optimize Web Vitals (LCP, CLS, FID) — test changes with Lighthouse or WebPageTest
- Watch for: unnecessary reactivity (`computed` vs `watch` vs `methods`), memory leaks (e.g., subscriptions without cleanup), overuse of watchers or deep reactivity, large DOM structures or slow templates

## Error Handling Patterns

```js
// Standard API error handling
try {
  await this.fetchData();
} catch (err) {
  if (!EXCLUDED_SERVER_ERROR_STATUSES.includes(err.status)) {
    this.$router.push({ name: ROUTES_NAMES.error });
  }
  console.error(err);
} finally {
  this.isLoading = false;
}
```

## Common Anti-Patterns to Avoid

```js
// ❌ Don't use function keyword for methods
function myMethod() {}

// ✅ Use arrow functions with const
const myMethod = () => {};

// ❌ Don't export default from .js files
export default myFunction;

// ✅ Use named exports
export const myFunction = () => {};

// ❌ Don't use v-model on form fields (except then this component is using in the same component with form ref)
<input v-model="form.name" />

// ✅ Use v-field directive
<c-name-field v-field="form.name" />

// ❌ Don't import globally registered components
import CNameField from '@/components/forms/fields/c-name-field.vue';

// ✅ Components starting with 'c-' are auto-registered
```

